      <floats-group id="article-24763.s13">
        <fig id="article-24763.image.f1" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Total Simple Mastectomy. This illustration&#x000a0;shows&#x000a0;a scheme for&#x000a0;removing the breast and lymph nodes in a total (simple) mastectomy. The dotted line shows where the breast incision is made. The procedure may also include axillary lymph node dissection. National Cancer Institute, <ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?search=Total+Simple+Mastectomy&#x00026;title=Special:MediaSearch&#x00026;go=Go&#x00026;type=image">Public Domain</ext-link>, via Wikimedia Commons&#x000a0;&#x000a0;</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Total_xsimplex_mastectomy" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-24763.s14">
        <fig id="article-24763.image.f2" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Modified Radical Mastectomy. Image of a modified radical mastectomy and axillary lymph node dissection, which uses an incision similar to the simple mastectomy but may extend into the axilla for simultaneous axillary lymphadenectomy. Contributed by S Munakomi, MD, and Ashis Pun, MD</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="ap13" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
